ces from 59% to 40% (−1.14).","12.d4 stops …Ncxe5.","Black was threatening …Ncxe5, winning a pawn. d4 takes care of it.","Black answers 12…d5."]},{"ply":52,"name":"26…Qf5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"26…Qf5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked queen to safety.","Lines up queen and bishop on the diagonal, so the two pieces push in the same direction.","The next best move, 26…Bxg4, would drop Black's winning chances from 49% to 29% (+2.49).","26…Qf5 threatens …Qxh5, winning the queen, and gets the queen out of the way of gxf4.","It threatens …Qxh5, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening gxf4, winning the queen for a pawn. The queen steps away."]},{"ply":67,"name":"34.Ba2","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"34.Ba2 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 48% to 22%.","points":["Black answers 34…Be5: adds a attacker on the knight on d4: 2 attackers against 2 defenders.","Better was 34.Nb3 (−0.20), which attacks the queen."],"better":"34.Nb3 threatens Nxc5, winning the queen for a rook, and stops …f4. Engine line: 34…Qd6 35.Ne3 Qe7 36.Nd5 Qd8 37.Nxc7 Qxc7 38.Nc5 (−0.20)."},{"ply":84,"name":"42…e3","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"42…e3!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the knight on d7 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: White's best reply is 43.Qd3 Qxd3 44.Nxd3 e2 instead (the engine rates it −6.53).","A pawn break: it challenges the f2 pawn to open lines.","42…e3 stops Qxd7 and threatens …Nc5, winning a pawn, picking it up with 44…exf2.","White was threatening Qxd7, winning a knight and a pawn. e3 takes care of it.","It threatens …Nc5, winning a pawn, picking it up with 44…exf2, so White has to spend the next move on it."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"42…e3!! leaves the knight on d7 hanging. Taking it gives Black the material back and more."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"The knight on d7 is attacked by the queen on d1, and 42…e3 doesn't save it. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"If White takes the knight","text":"43.Qxd7 is met by 43…exf2+, taking the f2 pawn with check. Then 44…Qxg3 takes the g3 pawn, and 45…Qg1+ gives check. Black ends up a piece ahead: Black is winning (−11.77)."},{"title":"White's best try","text":"White's best is to leave the knight alone and play 43.Qd3. Black answers 43…Qxd3, taking the queen on d3 and threatening …exf2+, winning a pawn. Then 44…e2 creates a passed pawn on e2, and 45…a6 pushes the passed pawn to a6. Black ends up two pawns ahead: Black is winning (−6.53)."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Sicilian Defense: Kramnik Variation","eco":"B40","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 4…Qb6: 8 moves in all, 4 by White and 4 by Black.","Theory runs out after 4…Qb6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}